External system requirements
============================

faissR uses a compatible installed FAISS library (>= 1.11.0) when available.
Otherwise it compiles the included FAISS 1.11.0 source into a static CPU library
with the active R toolchain. This needs no download, administrator access,
CMake, Python, or separate FAISS installation. CUDA/cuVS are not bundled.

The default is --configure-args='--with-faiss=auto'. Select bundled to force
the included source or system to require an installed FAISS library. The
equivalent environment variable is FAISSR_FAISS_SOURCE. A failed bundled build
is an error, not an automatic diagnostic-only installation.

NVIDIA CUDA and RAPIDS cuVS are not required for a CPU-only FAISS build. They
are required when the user explicitly requests the corresponding GPU features.

Required for all platforms
--------------------------

- R development headers.
- A C++20-capable compiler.
- A Fortran compiler for Unix functional builds. Windows uses the package's
  C++ exact fallback and does not compile the Fortran source.
- GNU make, OpenMP, and complete LP64 BLAS/LAPACK development libraries.
- Either installed FAISS or the included CPU source (selected automatically).

The Unix package shared library links against the LAPACK, BLAS, and Fortran
libraries reported by R. No separate BLAS implementation is required when R's
own BLAS/LAPACK libraries provide these symbols. Otherwise configure locates
complete numerical libraries, including single-precision routines, without
replacing R's BLAS. The bundled build uses portable CPU instructions rather
than forcing the build machine's instruction set on binary users.

The mandatory non-base R packages are `Rcpp`, `float`, and `gpuinfo`;
`methods` ships with R. R package installers normally resolve these dependencies
automatically. Building the vignettes additionally requires `knitr` and
`rmarkdown`.

If FAISS is not in a standard compiler/linker path or visible through
pkg-config, set FAISS_HOME to a prefix containing:

  include/faiss/IndexFlat.h
  lib/libfaiss.so      on Linux
  lib/libfaiss.a       on macOS or Windows (static libraries are required)

Example:

  FAISS_HOME=/path/to/faiss R CMD INSTALL .

Separate include and library directories can be supplied with the conventional
configure variables:

  R CMD INSTALL --configure-vars='INCLUDE_DIR=/path/include LIB_DIR=/path/lib' .

Linux CPU-only build
--------------------

On Debian or Ubuntu, the usual development packages are:

  sudo apt-get install libfaiss-dev libblas-dev liblapack-dev

libfaiss-dev is optional: when absent, faissR builds its included CPU source.
On Fedora, install gcc-c++, gcc-gfortran, make, blas-devel and lapack-devel;
the bundled fallback does not require a faiss-devel package. No package
installation invokes apt, dnf or another operating-system package manager.

After installing or building a CPU-only FAISS library, install faissR with:

  FAISS_HOME=/path/to/faiss-cpu \
  FAISSR_REQUIRE_FAISS=1 \
  FAISSR_USE_CUDA=0 \
  FAISSR_USE_CUVS=0 \
  R CMD INSTALL .

Linux NVIDIA GPU build
----------------------

Install a compatible NVIDIA driver, CUDA toolkit, FAISS build, and any RAPIDS
libraries required by the desired routes. Toolkit headers and libraries below
CUDA_HOME/targets/<platform>/ are detected alongside the conventional include,
lib, and lib64 directories.

Strict FAISS GPU / CUDA-native build:

  CUDA_HOME=/usr/local/cuda \
  FAISS_HOME=/path/to/faiss-gpu \
  FAISSR_REQUIRE_CUDA=1 \
  R CMD INSTALL .

Strict direct cuVS build:

  CUDA_HOME=/usr/local/cuda \
  FAISS_HOME=/path/to/faiss \
  CUVS_HOME=/path/to/rapids-or-cuvs \
  FAISSR_REQUIRE_CUDA=1 \
  FAISSR_REQUIRE_CUVS=1 \
  R CMD INSTALL .

The FAISSR_REQUIRE_* variables require the corresponding FAISS, CUDA, or cuVS
feature at configure time. If a required component is missing, installation
fails rather than producing a diagnostic or CPU-only build.
FAISSR_REQUIRE_CUDA=1 also requires a visible, usable GPU. An intentional
build-only host without an attached GPU can set
FAISSR_SKIP_CUDA_RUNTIME_CHECK=1, but that override does not validate CUDA
execution. FAISSR_REQUIRE_CUDA_RUNTIME is deprecated.

When a GPU is visible, configure detects the compute capabilities of all
visible devices and builds native code plus PTX for the detected architectures.
FAISSR_CUDA_ARCH and FAISSR_CUDA_PTX_ARCH remain available as expert overrides
for build-only hosts and cross-compilation.

macOS
-----

macOS builds are expected to be CPU/FAISS builds. NVIDIA CUDA is not supported
on modern macOS/Apple Silicon systems. Installation uses a compatible static
CPU FAISS library, including R-macos recipes prefixes under /opt/R/arm64 and
/opt/R/x86_64, or compiles the included CPU source. OpenMP and Fortran must
match the active R toolchain; the fallback does not supply those runtimes.

For a source build outside that environment, provide compatible FAISS and
OpenMP prefixes explicitly:

  FAISS_HOME=/path/to/faiss \
  LIBOMP_HOME=/path/to/openmp \
  FAISSR_REQUIRE_FAISS=1 \
  R CMD INSTALL .

The configure script does not invoke a package manager.

Windows
-------

faissR is eligible for native Windows builds and is not restricted by an
`OS_type` field. Normal installation uses a compatible installed static FAISS
or compiles the bundled CPU source with Rtools. Diagnostic-only installation
requires an explicit --with-faiss=none and is not a functional FAISS result.

Use the matching Rtools toolchain for a functional native CPU installation;
no separate FAISS build is necessary. WSL2 supports Linux-style builds.
Native RAPIDS cuVS C++ builds are generally Linux-oriented; for NVIDIA GPU
support on Windows, WSL2 is the recommended route.

Automated-builder notes
-----------------------

CRAN and r-universe CPU builders can use installed or bundled CPU FAISS.
CUDA/cuVS should not be required on CPU-only builders. If NVIDIA GPU support is
required for a particular build environment, use the strict FAISSR_REQUIRE_*
variables above.

The SystemRequirements field documents external libraries; R CMD INSTALL does
not install operating-system packages. Any new external requirements on CRAN
builders must be agreed with CRAN before submission. Debian's libfaiss-dev
can avoid rebuilding FAISS, but it is not needed for the bundled fallback.
No precompiled FAISS archive is shipped in the source package. Copyright,
license and patch information is in inst/COPYRIGHTS and src/vendor/README.

Functional verification
-----------------------

A production or CI installation should set FAISSR_REQUIRE_FAISS=1. After
installation, loading the package is not sufficient evidence of functionality;
run:

  Rscript -e 'library(faissR); stopifnot(faiss_available()); x <- matrix(rnorm(240), 40); z <- nn(x, 3, method="exact", backend="cpu"); stopifnot(identical(dim(z$indices), c(40L, 3L)))'

Windows diagnostic-only tests must explicitly use --with-faiss=none; the
normal Windows installation builds FAISS. WebAssembly remains diagnostic-only.
These profiles are not evidence of functional FAISS support.

The installed vignette gives pinned, tested commands, provider/toolchain
combinations, capability checks, license boundaries, and troubleshooting:

  Rscript -e 'vignette("installation", package="faissR")'

If package installation compiles but loading fails with an undefined BLAS
symbol such as `ssyrk_`, verify that the generated `src/Makevars` ends with
`$(LAPACK_LIBS) $(BLAS_LIBS) $(FLIBS)`. These libraries must follow `-lfaiss`
in the linker command.
